  "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}=5n-2\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n5(1)-2=3.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=5k-2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =5(k+1)-2 \\\\  & =5k+3.\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={a}_{k}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}+5 & =(5k-2)+5 \\\\  & =5k-2+5 \\\\  & =5k+3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}=5n-2$ is true for all $n\\in N.$",
